{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792313744,"nonce":"HSv6K3wZD3x7dU3RRU2TC9EakpU=","title":"Configure the build from a form, not text","message":"The user wants to configure a project's hxml from a GUI, not the code editor. A Build tab (a virtual tab in the editor area, as VS Code's Settings) edits the build file in place with form controls: target and output, main class, source folders, libraries (with installed ones offered), defines, compiler options (debug, dead code elimination, traces, warnings), and the lines the form does not manage, kept and listed. Edits go to the file at once, keeping its comments and order, and the compilation server and Display pick the change up. Entry points: the Explorer's build file, the command center, the welcome page, and a gear at the foot of the activity bar. A build with several targets (--next) edits the first and says so.\n\nNeeds: rayde.build.HxmlFile (parse, edit, save without losing comments), Display.reload, a virtual tab kind in the workbench, checks for the file model and a snapshot of the form in both themes.","files":null}]}